Article 6: In determining whether a network service provider 'knows or should know' pursuant to Article 1197 of the Civil Code, the People's Court shall comprehensively consider the following factors:
(1) whether the network service provider has processed the infringing network information by means of recommendation, ranking, selection, editing, organization, modification, or other means, whether manually or automatically;
(2) the network service provider's capability to manage information that it should possess, as well as the nature and manner of the services provided and the likelihood of causing infringement therefrom;
(3) the type and obviousness of the infringement on personal rights and interests by the network information;
(4) the degree of social impact of the network information or the number of page views within a certain period;
(5) the technical feasibility of the network service provider taking measures to prevent infringement and whether it has taken corresponding reasonable measures;
(6) whether the network service provider has taken corresponding reasonable measures against repeated infringements by the same network user or for the same infringing information;
(7) other factors related to the case.

Article 7: In determining the fault and the degree of fault of a network user or network service provider in reposting network information, the People's Court shall comprehensively consider the following factors:
(1) the duty of care borne by the reposting subject that is commensurate with its nature and scope of influence;
(2) the obviousness of the infringement on others' personal rights and interests by the reposted information;
(3) whether substantive modifications have been made to the reposted information, and whether the article title has been added or modified, resulting in a serious inconsistency with the content and the possibility of misleading the public.

Article 9: Where a network user or network service provider publishes information based on information sources such as documents produced by state organs in the exercise of their authority and acts performed by state organs in the public exercise of their authority, and any of the following circumstances exists, resulting in infringement on others' personal rights and interests, and the infringed person requests the infringer to bear tort liability, the People's Court shall support it:
(1) the information published by the network user or network service provider is inconsistent with the content of the aforesaid information sources;
(2) the network user or network service provider causes misunderstanding by adding insulting content, defamatory information, improper titles, or by adding or deleting information, adjusting the structure, changing the order, or other means;
(3) the aforesaid information source has been publicly corrected, but the network user refuses to correct it or the network service provider fails to correct it;
(4) after the aforesaid information source has been publicly corrected, the network user or network service provider still publishes the information before the correction.
